---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="mdbase Connect"
  description="mdbase Connect lets apps use your Markdown collections as their database. You approve each app, and your records stay as files you own, synced across your devices."
  current="connect"
>
  <header class="page-intro">
    <h1>mdbase Connect</h1>
    <p class="page-intro__lede">
      Connect lets apps use your Markdown collections as their database. An app
      doesn’t keep your data in its own storage. It asks for access to one of
      your collections, then finds, creates and edits records there, and
      every change is saved as a Markdown file you own.
    </p>
  </header>

  <section class="content-section" aria-labelledby="how-it-works">
    <h2 id="how-it-works">How it works</h2>
    <p>
      Say you keep your tasks in a collection. Each task is a Markdown file,
      like <code>tasks/renew-passport.md</code>:
    </p>
    <pre><code>---
type: task
title: Renew passport
status: open
due: 2026-12-01
---

Book a photo appointment first.</code></pre>
    <p>
      Connect <a href="/apps/">TaskNotes</a> to that collection and it queries
      the records of type <code>task</code> to build its lists. Mark this one
      done and TaskNotes changes <code>status</code> in the file. Edit the due
      date in a text editor and TaskNotes shows the new date. Any other app you
      connect to the same collection sees the same records, so nothing has to
      be exported from one app to another.
    </p>
    <p>
      Connect is the account that makes this work. It handles sign-in, the
      permissions you grant, syncing between your devices, and sharing with
      other people.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section" aria-labelledby="apps">
    <h2 id="apps">Apps ask first</h2>
    <p>
      When you connect an app, you sign in to Connect, choose one collection,
      and see the operations the app asks for before you approve it. You might
      let a project planner find, create and edit project records while leaving
      deletion blocked.
    </p>
    <p>
      The permission is recorded with the collection, and every copy checks it:
      your devices and, for Cloud copy, mdbase’s copy. You can narrow or revoke
      a permission at any time. Removing an app never removes the collection,
      and your other apps keep using it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section" aria-labelledby="sync-options">
    <h2 id="sync-options">Cloud copy or Private</h2>
    <p>
      Connect keeps each collection in sync across your devices. Every device
      that syncs it keeps a complete copy as ordinary files and keeps working
      offline. For each collection, you choose whether mdbase can read it:
    </p>
    <dl class="glossary-list">
      <div>
        <dt>Cloud copy</dt>
        <dd>
          The default. mdbase keeps a readable copy of the collection, so web
          apps keep working while your devices are off, and a new device joins
          when you sign in.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Private</dt>
        <dd>
          End-to-end encrypted. Only your devices hold the key, so mdbase can’t
          read the collection. Apps reach it through one of your devices, so one
          of them must be online, such as a computer running the
          <a href="/downloads/">desktop app</a>.
        </dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
    <p>
      The <a href="/security/">security page</a> lists exactly what mdbase
      stores and can read in each case.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section" aria-labelledby="devices">
    <h2 id="devices">Add a device</h2>
    <p>
      For a Cloud copy collection, sign in on the new device and it downloads
      the collection. For a Private collection, you also unlock it with your
      encryption password or recovery key, or, in strict mode, approve it from
      a device you already have. See
      <a href="/security/#private-keys">Private collection keys</a> for how
      this works.
    </p>
    <p>
      If you lose a device, remove it from your account. It receives no further
      changes but keeps the files it already had.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section" aria-labelledby="share-a-collection">
    <h2 id="share-a-collection">Share a collection</h2>
    <p>
      Owners can invite people as viewers, who can read, or editors, who can
      read and change records. Both Cloud copy and Private collections can be
      shared. Open the collection’s sharing controls in the
      <a href="https://editor.mdbase.dev/">mdbase Editor</a> and invite someone
      by email. They need a registered, verified Connect account.
    </p>
    <p>
      Each member’s devices keep a full copy, and members can connect their own
      apps within the role you gave them. In a Private collection, an owner or
      editor approves a new member’s first device by comparing a six-digit code
      with them, in person or on a call.
    </p>
    <p>
      You can change a member’s role or remove them at any time. A removed
      member stops receiving changes and keeps the files they already had.
    </p>
    <p>
      Sharing covers a whole collection. To share part of one, make a separate
      collection. Edits that people make at the same time are combined field by
      field, and edits that change the same field are held for review. There is
      no live co-editing of a note.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section" aria-labelledby="get-started">
    <h2 id="get-started">Get started</h2>
    <p>
      <a href="/beta/">Create a Connect account</a>,{" "}
      <a href="/downloads/">download the desktop app</a>, or{" "}
      <a href="https://connect.mdbase.dev/login">sign in</a> to manage an
      existing account. See the <a href="/apps/">apps</a> that work with mdbase
      collections.
    </p>
    <p>
      Building an app? <a href="/sdk/connect-quickstart/">Connect a browser
      app</a> walks through sign-in, choosing a collection and working with
      records using the <a href="/sdk/">SDK</a>.
    </p>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
